---
import { GITHUB_URL, LINKEDIN_URL } from '../consts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
	title="About"
	description="Jagnyesh Patra works on clinical data standards, FHIR and OMOP pipelines, and testing AI output against clinical data standards."
	narrow
>
	<article class="prose">
		<h1>About</h1>
		<p>
			I'm Jagnyesh Patra. I work at the Tufts Clinical and Translational Science Institute, where I evaluate AI tools
			for development and data discovery. I also built a self-paced trainer that takes SQL analysts through OMOP data.
			OMOP is the common data model that research networks use to put EHR data from different hospitals into one
			shape.
		</p>
		<p>
			For nine years I have worked where clinical documentation and payer data meet the systems that run on them. That
			work has been at providers, payers, a pharmacy benefit manager, and the CDC. At Optum I set the mapping
			standards for turning HL7 v2 messages into FHIR resources, and 8 business units adopted them. HL7 v2 is the
			older message format most hospital systems still send. FHIR is the current HL7 standard for exchanging health
			records. I also led development of Patient Access APIs certified for USCDI v1, the federal list of data elements that
			certified health IT must exchange.
		</p>
		<p>
			At the CDC I worked on federal and state data interoperability during COVID-19 and contributed to USCDI data
			element proposals. At the University of Illinois Chicago I designed an OMOP data layer that put data from
			several EHR systems into one standard representation.
		</p>

		<h2>From product to building</h2>
		<p>
			Most of that work was in product and analyst roles. I interviewed clinicians and principal investigators, wrote
			requirements, prioritized backlogs, and launched MVPs. I still start there, and now I write the code and the
			evaluations as well. The <a href="/projects/">projects</a> on this site are where I do that in public, on synthetic
			data only.
		</p>

		<h2>How I work</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				I measure a claim against a baseline and publish the result when it goes against me. In
				<a href="/projects/#rcm-ai">rcm-ai</a>, the plain-LLM baseline beat my knowledge graph on accuracy, and the
				README says so first.
			</li>
			<li>I keep every fact traceable to the source row it came from.</li>
			<li>I put human review where a wrong answer costs the most.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
		<p>
			My <a href="/resume/">résumé</a> has the full work history. The code is on <a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>, and
			I'm on <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a>.
		</p>
	</article>
</Base>
